  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThere is high confidence in continued warm and dry conditions \npersisting through Saturday under ridging and surface high \npressure. High clouds are forecast to increase late Saturday\ninto Sunday as high cirrus fan out from an Atlantic coastal low.\n\nAt this point, there is high confidence the coastal low moves\nfrom the Mid-Altlatic to the Northeast on Sunday, with some\nreasonable confidence that the bulk of precipitation remains to\nthe east of Pittsburgh. Cluster median QPF is zero through \nSunday across the forecast area in all but one scenario where \nthe low track is farther west. Even in this event, median QPF \nin eastern Tucker is 0.25\", with a reasonable worst case \nscenario of 1\"-2\" in the WV/PA ridges, which would not amount to\nmuch of a flood concern given antecedent dry conditions. \n\nThe low will be shunted east by a cold front, which is expected\nto pass early Monday. This will bring a chance of showers (90th\npercentile only ~0.15\"), and cooler temperatures that will\nlinger through next week with a troughing pattern.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLow, but non-zero winter weather chances linger around\nThanksgiving. At this time, we will still be in the \"monitoring\"\nphase as there is still considerable uncertainty in how this \nunfolds. Lets talk about blanket probabilities first: 50% of NBM\nensembles have no snow in the area in this period, 25% have a\nlight accumulation in high terrain with near-advisory criteria\nin eastern Tucker County, and only 10% have widespread\naccumulation across the Allegheny Plateau. \n\nSo, why is there some much spread? Well, there is still poor\nagreement in the general pattern. 500mb heights show anything \nfrom zonal flow, to a closed low, to a deep mid-west trough.\nPatterns that bring widespread snow will generally rely on a\ndeep trough developing, which wraps up a southeast low into the\nnortheast with strong cyclogenesis. Again, at this time,\nwidespread non-zero accumulations are only ~10% likely, so we \nwill have to continue to keep an eye on the pattern. The \neventual low development is dependent on a shortwave on a PNW \nlow that will develop the next couple days. Once this resolves \nbetter, we may know more.",
